# <Phase>

One-line name of the implementation phase or capability being delivered.

## Goal

State the concrete outcome this phase should achieve and why it matters.

## Scope

Define what is included, excluded, and constrained in this phase.

## Design

Describe the key architecture, contracts, flows, and design decisions that guide the implementation.

## Changes

Summarize the files, components, interfaces, and behaviors that will be created, modified, or removed.

## E2E Tests

Define the user-visible end-to-end scenarios that prove the phase works as intended.

### Happy Path

Cover the primary successful workflow from start to finish.

### Important Variants

Cover meaningful alternative inputs, contexts, modes, or supported usage patterns.

### Error Paths

Cover expected failures and verify useful behavior, messages, and exit states.

### Help / Command Discovery

Verify that new commands, options, documentation, or entry points are discoverable and correct.

### Regression Coverage

Verify that existing critical workflows continue to work after the change.

## Verification

List the final technical and functional checks required before the phase is considered complete.

## Implementation Steps

Break the work into ordered, independently understandable implementation outcomes.

### Step 1: <Implementation Outcome>

Describe one coherent implementation outcome rather than a single low-level file edit.

#### Changes

Identify the concrete codebase changes required for this step.

##### Create

List new files, components, types, tests, or other artifacts to add.

##### Modify

List existing files, components, interfaces, or behavior to update.

##### Remove

List obsolete code, files, behavior, or compatibility paths to delete when applicable.

#### Implementation

Describe exactly how the required behavior should be implemented.

##### <Component / Behavior 1>

Detail the first significant implementation area or behavior.

##### <Component / Behavior 2>

Detail the next significant implementation area or behavior.

##### Error Handling

Define how expected failures should be detected, propagated, and presented.

##### Output / UX

Define user-visible output, interaction behavior, formatting, or API semantics where relevant.

#### Patterns & Constraints

Capture implementation decisions and boundaries the implementer should not have to rediscover.

##### Mirror

Point to existing code, architecture, or conventions that should be followed.

##### Decisions

Record important choices that constrain how the implementation should work.

##### Gotchas

Call out non-obvious edge cases, dependencies, or failure modes that could cause mistakes.

##### Out of Scope

Clarify tempting adjacent work that must not be introduced in this step.

#### Tests

Specify the automated coverage required for this implementation step.

##### E2E

Identify the end-to-end scenarios enabled or affected by this step.

##### Integration

Verify interactions between components, layers, adapters, services, or boundaries.

##### Unit

Cover isolated logic where focused tests provide useful value beyond integration or E2E coverage.

#### Validation

Define how the completed step can be checked immediately and objectively.

##### Commands

List the build, test, lint, type-check, or manual commands used to validate the step.

##### Expected Results

State the observable conditions that confirm the step was implemented correctly.

### Step n: <Implementation Outcome>

Repeat the same structure for each remaining coherent implementation outcome.

#### Changes

Summarize what changes in this step.

#### Implementation

Explain the required behavior and implementation details.

#### Patterns & Constraints

Capture conventions, decisions, gotchas, and boundaries relevant to this step.

#### Tests

Define the E2E, integration, and unit coverage needed for this step.

#### Validation

State how to execute and confirm that this step is complete.
